From 651d3b79e61f72684b426804754e03e204ae4aca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jakob Linke <jgruber@chromium.org>
Date: Fri, 8 May 2026 10:27:48 +0200
Subject: [Backport] Dependency for CVE-2026-113044

Manual backort of patch originally reviewed on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/v8/v8/+/7817342:
Internalize 2-byte strings with one-byte content as one-byte

Internalization picked the encoding from the source string's map, so a
SLICED_TWO_BYTE or CONS_TWO_BYTE with all-ASCII content was internalized
as INTERNALIZED_TWO_BYTE. This poisons JS3 json-stringify-inspector when
postcss-wtb runs first: postcss makes 2-byte slices of a CSS source that
contains non-ASCII bytes, autoprefixer interns the result, and around 39
CSS property names end up as 2-byte interned entries. JSON.parse later
reuses those entries, FastJsonStringifier flips into the 2-byte template
the first time it sees one, and the rest of the run is ~35% slower.

Make internalization content-aware by reusing the hasher's existing
IsOnly8Bit pass: StringTable::LookupString threads its result through
InternalizedStringKey to NewInternalizedStringImpl and
ComputeInternalizationStrategyForString, which then allocate a
SeqOneByteString instead of locking in the wider representation. The
same answer gates the external-2-byte branch in PrepareForInsertion, so
external 2-byte ASCII (Blink's fetch().text() output) canonicalizes too.
If the hash was computed before internalization we don't rescan; the
threaded value stays false and we fall back to the existing map-keyed
path.

JS3 (d8, 3 runs, x64.release):
  json-stringify-inspector  +53.2% / +96.2% First
  json-parse-inspector      +16.1%
  source-map-wtb             +8.4%
  delta-blue Worst          +12.2%
  richards                   +7.2%

